WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.760
When I was a student, in fact when I was at high school,

00:00:04.760 --> 00:00:10.720
we had to do analytical chemistry, trying to identify different ions

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:13.900
like bromide, iodide, chloride,

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:15.720
and we did all sorts of tests.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:20.240
So I thought it'd be quite fun to get Sam to do one of these tests.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.280
These tests look really quite pretty,

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.100
though when I was a schoolboy I found them really tedious.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:33.800
It involves adding silver nitrate to the solutions and you get a precipitate.

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:38.400
When you add the silver nitrate, you get silver ions in the solution

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:43.020
which also contains nitrate, because you put in silver nitrate,

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:46.540
but also chloride or bromide or iodide ions.

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:53.400
The silver halides are very insoluble, so when the silver and the halide ions meet,

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.900
they form a solid and precipitate.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:05.980
So, there's quite a nice reaction and you get a precipitate of different colours depending which halogen you use.

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:13.160
So, as a test, although I didn't mention this to Sam, she decided she would use fluoride as well

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:16.540
That's all four halogens that you can get easily.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:23.480
Astatine and the new one, tennessine, aren't really available to do lab experiments.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:29.340
Silver ions with fluoride was a bit disappointing.

00:01:29.740 --> 00:01:34.960
Silver fluoride is reasonably soluble in water, so you don't form a precipitate.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:38.620
With silver chloride, you get a fairly white precipitate

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.640
and these precipitation reactions take place incredibly fast,

00:01:43.820 --> 00:01:46.440
much faster than your eye can follow.

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:50.080
This is not something that gradually develops.

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:52.800
One minute it's clear, the next minute BOOM!

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:55.500
Silver bromide is pale yellow

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:01.060
and silver iodide is rather more yellow.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.540
[soothing music plays]

00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:27.360
And why am I telling you about these compounds?

00:02:27.500 --> 00:02:34.860
And the reason is that these silver halides have been technologically very important,

00:02:34.860 --> 00:02:44.120
particularly silver bromide, because silver bromide was the material that was used for old-fashioned photographic film.

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:48.000
Nowadays, everybody takes digital pictures

00:02:48.000 --> 00:02:53.700
but, until really quite recently, most photographs were taken on film

00:02:53.780 --> 00:02:59.660
and the film has a layer of silver bromide crystals, very small ones,

00:02:59.940 --> 00:03:03.480
and the amazing thing about silver bromide crystals

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:10.080
is that, when they absorb light, they are dissociated

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.020
so you get silver metal formed.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.040
You don't get very much silver metal formed,

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:21.160
but then, when you put it into developing solution,

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:27.240
that tiny piece of silver acts as a catalyst and bigger particles grow

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:30.740
to give you the black colour on black and white film.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:37.580
I hope one day we will be able to show you in a darkroom the chemistry of film

00:03:37.580 --> 00:03:43.760
but this compound that's sitting in the test tube is the basis of all photographic film.

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.100
There is one quite funny question which people sometimes ask

00:03:48.100 --> 00:03:53.220
which is:  What is the colour of unexposed photographic film?

00:03:53.860 --> 00:03:57.660
That is, film which has not been exposed to light.

00:03:58.600 --> 00:04:03.980
Because, if you want to know the colour of something, you have to shine light on it.

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:08.940
And if you shine light on it, the film has been exposed.

00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:14.180
So nobody really knows what the colour of unexposed film is.

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:21.340
And silver iodide crystals have a really unexpected application,

00:04:22.180 --> 00:04:24.060
which is to make rain.

00:04:24.140 --> 00:04:27.300
In the sky, there's a lot of water vapour,

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:32.980
and the water vapour can condense to form clouds, and the clouds produce rain.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.920
The problem is that water vapour has to be cooled to a very low temperature

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:41.360
before it will spontaneously form crystals of ice

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:45.500
but if there are tiny particles in the air,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:50.740
this acts as a nucleus, and the water droplets or ice particles can form round them.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:54.860
Shortly after the Second World War, about 70 years ago,

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.880
it was suddenly realized that the structure of the crystals of silver iodide

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:05.080
have the same sort of spacing of the atoms as in crystals of ice

00:05:05.720 --> 00:05:11.260
so people had the idea of injecting very fine powders of silver iodide

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:17.640
into the air where clouds were forming to make them form thicker clouds, and therefore to rain.

00:05:17.780 --> 00:05:22.000
And it works, but nobody quite knows how

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:29.140
and most recently people have done the experiments and show that ice doesn't form in the way that people thought

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.340
and so the spacing of the atoms on the silver iodide

00:05:33.340 --> 00:05:37.800
is not the relevant property, but it still seems to work.

00:05:38.080 --> 00:05:44.580
And so, in many countries now, people use silver iodide for seeding clouds.

00:05:48.000 --> 00:05:50.800
There are two reasons for seeding clouds:

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:57.160
One reason is to make it rain, to make your plants grow better.

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:06.700
The other reason is to get the clouds to start producing rain before a real storm starts.

00:06:07.240 --> 00:06:11.880
If you don't seed the clouds, then they build up and up and up

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:15.280
and then suddenly start producing large hailstones

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:20.220
which can cause enormous damage, smashing buildings, denting cars, and so on.

00:06:21.520 --> 00:06:29.020
So silver iodide can reduce, or people believe that it can reduce,

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.780
the force of a storm, and cause less damage underneath.

